 cause, state exactly\nwhat additional information or logs are needed — never guess.\nIf you are not certain of the root cause, say so explicitly.\n\nISSUE DESCRIPTION, ERROR OUTPUT, AND CODE CONTEXT:\n[Paste error messages, stack traces, logs, code snippets, reproduction steps,\nand any relevant system context here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Do not suggest a fix before identifying the root cause\n- Fix must be minimal and targeted — do not refactor unrelated code\n- Every fix must include an explanation of why it works\n- Regression tests must cover the exact scenario that caused the bug\n- Performance fixes must include before/after metrics or measurement approach\n- Production fixes must consider rollback strategy\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nDEBUGGING AND OPTIMIZATION REPORT\nProject: [Project Name] | Issue Type: [Type] | Severity: [Severity]\nPlatform: [Platform] | Environment: [Environment]\nDiagnosed By: [Engineer Name] | Date: [DD/MM/YYYY]\n\n---\n\n1. ISSUE CLASSIFICATION:\nType: Bug | Performance | Memory Leak | Crash | Race Condition | Security | Build\nOrigin Layer: Frontend | Backend | Database | Network | Infrastructure | Unknown\nAffected Component: [Specific component, service, or function]\nUser Impact: [What the user experiences]\nReproducible: Always | Intermittent ([N] of [N] attempts) | Unable to reproduce yet\n\n---\n\n2. HYPOTHESIS FORMATION:\n| # | Hypothesis | Likelihood | Reasoning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [Hypothesis] | High/Med/Low | [Why this is plausible] |\n| 2 | [Hypothesis] | High/Med/Low | [Why this is plausible] |\n| 3 | [Hypothesis] | High/Med/Low | [Why this is plausible] |\n\nInvestigation order: Start with Hypothesis [N] — [reason why highest likelihood]\n\n---\n\n3. DIAGNOSTIC APPROACH:\nSTEP 1 — [Diagnostic step title]\nAction: [What to check or run]\nCommand / Tool: [Exact command, query, or tool]\nPositive result: [What it means — go to Resolution]\nNegative result: [What it means — proceed to Step 2]\n\nSTEP 2 — [Diagnostic step title]\nAction: [What to check or run]\nCommand / Tool: [Exact command or tool]\nPositive result: [Meaning]\nNegative result: [Meaning — proceed to Step 3]\n\n---\n(add steps as needed)\n\n---\n\n4. ROOT CAUSE IDENTIFICATION:\nRoot Cause: [Confirmed underlying cause — one clear sentence]\nEvidence: [What in the logs, code, or output confirms this]\nWhy it happened:\n  Why 1: [First level cause]\n  Why 2: [Deeper cause]\n  Why 3: [Root cause]\n\nContributing factors:\n  - [Factor 1]\n  - [Factor 2]\n\n---\n\n5. FIX IMPLEMENTATION:\nBEFORE (buggy code):\nFile: [filename.ext]\n[Original problematic code]\n\nProblem: [Exactly what is wrong and why]\n\nAFTER (fixed code):\nFile: [filename.ext]\n[Complete fixed production-ready code]\n\nWhy this fix works: [Explanation]\n\nAdditional changes required:\n| File | Change | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| [filename] | [Change] | [Why needed] |\n\nRollback plan: [How to revert if fix causes issues in production]\n\n---\n\n6. OPTIMIZATION OPPORTUNITIES:\n| Opportunity | Current State | Optimized Approach | Expected Improvement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Opportunity] | [Current] | [Optimized] | [Expected gain] |\n\nPriority: Address in [same PR / next sprint / backlog] — justification: [Why]\n\n---\n\n7. REGRESSION PREVENTION:\nTest for the bug:\nFile: [filename.test.ext]\n[Complete test covering exact failure scenario]\n\nAdditional test cases:\n| Test | Scenario | Expected Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| [Test name] | [Scenario] | [Expected result] |\n\nMonitoring to add:\n| Alert | Condition | Channel | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Alert] | [Condition] | [Channel] | [Role] |\n\n---\n\n8. PERFORMANCE ANALYSIS:\n(Complete only if Issue Type is Performance or Memory Leak)\n\n| Tool | What to Measure | How to Run |\n|---|---|---|\n| [Tool] | [Metric] | [Command / steps] |\n\n| Metric | Before | Target | After Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Metric] | [Value] | [Target] | [Value] |\n\n---\n\nINFORMATION NEEDED (if root cause unconfirmed):\n| Information | Why Needed | How to Obtain |\n|---|---|---|\n| [What is needed] | [Why] | [Exact steps] |
28	7	Reliability and incident analysis	ROLE:\nYou are a DevOps Engineer and Incident Manager documenting a production incident\nfor a Native iOS/Android mobile application.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nApp Version Affected: [Version]\nFixed In Version: [Version — or "Fix in progress"]\nPlatform Affected: iOS | Android | Both\nIncident Severity: P1 Critical | P2 High | P3 Medium | P4 Low\nReported At: [DD/MM/YYYY HH:MM TZ]\nResolved At: [DD/MM/YYYY HH:MM TZ — or "Ongoing"]\nDetected By: User Report | Monitoring Alert | App Store Review | Internal Discovery\nIncident Commander: [Role]\n\nTASK:\nUsing the raw incident notes below, produce a complete incident report.\nCover: timeline, user impact, root cause, immediate fix, permanent fix, and prevention.\nWrite a separate executive summary suitable for senior stakeholders — plain English,\nno technical jargon, maximum 4 sentences.\nDo NOT assign blame to individuals — use role titles only.\nDo NOT omit the negative consequences or gaps that allowed this incident to occur.\nRoot cause must be a single confirmed cause — not a list of possibilities.\n\nRAW INCIDENT NOTES:\n[Paste Slack thread, war room notes, engineer findings, or timeline bullets here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Timeline must be strictly chronological with exact times where known\n- Estimated times must be labelled as "~[HH:MM]" not presented as exact\n- Impact must quantify affected users or percentage of user base where data exists\n- Prevention measures must be concrete, assigned actions — not generic advice\n- Every action item must have a named owner role and target date\n- If this was a third-party / dependency failure, that must be stated explicitly\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nINCIDENT REPORT\nID: INC-[Number] | Severity: [P1/P2/P3/P4] | Status: Resolved | Ongoing\nPlatform: [iOS/Android/Both] | App Version: [Affected] → [Fixed]\nIncident Commander: [Role] | Report Date: [DD/MM/YYYY]\n\n\nEXECUTIVE SUMMARY (for stakeholders — plain English, max 4 sentences)\n[What happened, who was affected, what was done, and current status]\n\n\nINCIDENT TIMELINE\n[HH:MM TZ] — [Event — e.g., Monitoring alert triggered for elevated crash rate]\n[HH:MM TZ] — [Event — e.g., On-call engineer notified]\n[HH:MM TZ] — [Event — e.g., Root cause identified]\n[HH:MM TZ] — [Event — e.g., Hotfix deployed to production]\n[HH:MM TZ] — [Event — e.g., Incident resolved — error rate returned to baseline]\n\nTOTAL DURATION: [X hours Y minutes]\nTime to Detect: [N minutes] | Time to Respond: [N minutes] | Time to Resolve: [N minutes]\n\n\nIMPACT ASSESSMENT\nUsers Affected: [Number or % of active user base]\nFeatures Unavailable: [List affected features]\nData Loss: Yes — [description] | No\nRevenue Impact: [Estimated or "Not applicable"]\nSLA Breach: Yes | No | Under Review\n\nROOT CAUSE\nPrimary Cause: [Single confirmed root cause]\nContributing Factors:\n  - [Factor 1 — conditions that allowed the root cause to have impact]\n  - [Factor 2]\nThird-Party Involvement: Yes — [Vendor/Service] | No\n\n\nRESOLUTION\nImmediate Fix (Hotfix):\n  Action: [What was done to stop the bleeding]\n  Deployed: [DD/MM/YYYY HH:MM] | Version: [X.X.X]\n  App Store / Play Store Submission: Required | Not Required\n\nPermanent Fix:\n  Action: [What was changed to prevent recurrence]\n  Deployed: [DD/MM/YYYY HH:MM] | Version: [X.X.X — or "Scheduled: Sprint N"]\n\nPREVENTION MEASURES\n| Action | Owner | Due Date | JIRA Ticket |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Concrete preventive action] | [Role] | [DD/MM/YYYY] | [ID or "To be raised"] |\n\nMONITORING IMPROVEMENTS:\n  - [Alert or monitoring change to catch this class of issue earlier]\n  (or "Existing monitoring was sufficient — detection time acceptable")\n\nPROCESS IMPROVEMENTS:\n  - [Change to deployment process, testing, or review to prevent recurrence]\n\n\nPOST-MORTEM SCHEDULED\nDate: [DD/MM/YYYY] | Attendees: [Roles] | Facilitator: [Role]\n(or "Post-mortem not required for this severity level")\n\nRELATED JIRA TICKETS: [IDs — or "None"]\nPREVIOUS SIMILAR INCIDENTS: [INC-NNN — or "None identified"]
12	3	Data interpretation summaries	ROLE:\nYou are an experienced Business Analyst / Data Analyst responsible for producing\nstructured, insight-driven Data Interpretation Summaries that translate raw data\nand metrics into clear, actionable findings for business and technical stakeholders.\n\nCONTEXT:\nOrganisation: [Company / Team Name]\nProject / Initiative: [Project Name or Business Area]\nReport Period: [e.g., Sprint 14 | Week ending DD/MM/YYYY | Q3 FY2025]\nData Source(s): [e.g., GA4 | Mixpanel | SQL export | Excel | CRM | App Store Connect]\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Web | All | Internal System\nAudience: [e.g., C-Suite | Product Team | Engineering | Client Stakeholder]\nAnalysis Type: Descriptive | Diagnostic | Predictive | Prescriptive\nComplexity: Low | Medium | High\nBusiness Context: [e.g., Post-launch review | Sprint retrospective data |\n                   Quarterly business review | Feature performance review]\n\nTASK:\nUsing the data inputs and business context provided below, produce a structured\nData Interpretation Summary that moves beyond raw numbers to deliver clear\nfindings, root cause analysis, and prioritised recommendations.\n\nYou MUST cover ALL of the following — do not skip any:\n  1. Executive summary — key findings in plain language for senior stakeholders\n  2. Data overview — what data was analysed, its source, quality, and limitations\n  3. Key metrics — performance against targets with trend analysis\n  4. Findings and insights — what the data is telling us and why it matters\n  5. Root cause analysis — diagnosis of underperformance or anomalies\n  6. Segment and cohort analysis — how performance varies across groups\n  7. Risks and flags — data-driven signals that require attention or action\n  8. Recommendations — prioritised, specific, and owned actions\n  9. Open questions — data gaps or decisions that need resolution\n  10. Next review — what to measure next and when\n\nEvaluate the FULL data context before drawing conclusions.\nEvery finding must be supported by a specific data point — do not make\nassertions without evidence.\nEvery recommendation must include an owner and a timeframe — do not list\nsuggestions without assigning accountability.\nFlag any area where data is insufficient to draw a reliable conclusion\nas [DATA GAP].\nFlag any metric where the target is unknown or undefined as [TARGET NEEDED].\n\nDATA INPUTS AND BUSINESS CONTEXT:\n[Paste raw data, exported metrics, dashboard screenshots description, SQL\nresults, or summary tables here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Findings must be specific — no vague statements like "engagement dropped"\n  (state by how much, over what period, compared to what baseline)\n- Recommendations must be realistic and actionable within the team's control\n- Do not present correlation as causation — flag where causation is unconfirmed\n- Segment analysis must cover at least platform, user type, or time period\n  where data allows\n- Risks must include both likelihood and potential business impact\n- Do not repeat the same insight under different headings\n- Audience tone must match the stated audience — adjust technical depth accordingly\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n\nDATA INTERPRETATION SUMMARY\nProject / Area: [Name] | Period: [Report Period] | Platform: [Platform]\nPrepared By: [Analyst Name] | Date: [DD/MM/YYYY] | Audience: [Audience]\n\n---\n\n1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY:\n  [3–5 sentences maximum. Written for a senior non-technical audience.\n  Cover: what was measured, the single most important finding, and the\n  top recommended action. No jargon. No raw numbers without context.]\n\nTop 3 Takeaways:\n  - [Takeaway 1 — one sentence, plain language, most critical finding]\n  - [Takeaway 2 — one sentence]\n  - [Takeaway 3 — one sentence]\n\n---\n\n2. DATA OVERVIEW:\nData Sources Used:\n| Source | Data Type | Period Covered | Volume / Records | Extraction Method |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., GA4] | [e.g., Web sessions] | [DD/MM – DD/MM/YYYY] | [e.g., 42,000 sessions] | [e.g., CSV export / API] |\n| [Source 2] | ... | ... | ... | ... |\n\nData Quality Assessment:\n| Issue | Affected Metric | Severity | Action Taken |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., 3-day tracking gap — iOS 17 update] | [e.g., Session count] | High/Med/Low | [e.g., Excluded from trend line / flagged] |\n| [e.g., Null values in user_id field — 4%] | [e.g., Cohort analysis] | Med | [e.g., Treated as anonymous users] |\n\nLimitations:\n  - [DATA GAP] [e.g., "No offline conversion data available — online metrics\n    only. Impact: revenue attribution may be understated."]\n  - [DATA GAP] [e.g., "Android data only available from DD/MM — pre-date\n    comparisons are iOS only."]\n\n---\n\n3. KEY METRICS:\n| Metric | Target | Actual | vs Target | vs Prior Period | Trend | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., DAU] | [TARGET NEEDED] / [X] | [Y] | [+/- Z%] | [+/- Z%] | ↑ ↓ → | 🟢 On Track / 🟡 At Risk / 🔴 Off Track |\n| [e.g., Session Duration] | [X mins] | [Y mins] | [-Z%] | [+Z%] | ↓ | 🟡 At Risk |\n| [e.g., Crash Rate] | [<X%] | [Y%] | [+Z%] | [+Z%] | ↑ | 🔴 Off Track |\n| [e.g., Conversion Rate] | [X%] | [Y%] | [+Z%] | [-Z%] | → | 🟢 On Track |\n\nMetric Notes:\n  - [TARGET NEEDED] [e.g., "No retention target has been set — recommend\n    defining a D7 and D30 benchmark before next review"]\n  - [e.g., "Crash rate spike correlates with v2.3 release on DD/MM —\n    see Root Cause Analysis section"]\n\n---\n\n4. FINDINGS AND INSIGHTS:\n[Each finding must cite a specific metric or data point. Group related\nfindings. Do not list raw numbers — interpret what they mean.]\n\nFinding 1: [Short descriptive title — e.g., "Onboarding drop-off\n           concentrated at Step 3"]\n  - Data: [e.g., "62% of new users exit at the permissions screen —\n    up from 41% in the prior period"]\n  - Significance: [Why this matters to the business or product]\n  - Confidence: High | Medium | Low — [reason if not High]\n  - Related Metric(s): [Metric names from Section 3]\n\nFinding 2: [Short descriptive title]\n  - Data: [Specific data point]\n  - Significance: [Business impact]\n  - Confidence: High | Medium | Low\n  - Related Metric(s): [Metric names]\n\n[Continue for all significant findings — do not pad with minor observations]\n\n---\n\n5. ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS:\n[Only complete this section for underperforming or anomalous metrics.\nDo not assert causation without evidence — use "likely", "correlated with",\nor "hypothesis" where causation is unconfirmed.]\n\n| Metric / Finding | Observed Symptom | Likely Root Cause | Evidence | Confidence | Causation Confirmed? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., Crash Rate ↑] | [e.g., 3x spike from DD/MM] | [e.g., Memory leak introduced in v2.3 build] | [e.g., Crash logs + release timing correlation] | High/Med/Low | Yes / No — [if No, state what is needed to confirm] |\n| [e.g., Retention D7 ↓] | [e.g., -18% vs prior month] | [e.g., Hypothesis: push notification opt-out rate increased after permission prompt change] | [e.g., Opt-out rate rose from 22% to 51% same period] | Medium | No — [A/B test needed to confirm] |\n\n---\n\n6. SEGMENT AND COHORT ANALYSIS:\n[Break down key metrics by meaningful segments. Use the segments most\nrelevant to the data available — do not leave this section generic.]\n\nBy Platform:\n| Metric | iOS | Android | Web | Variance | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Metric] | [Value] | [Value] | [Value] | [+/-Z%] | [e.g., "Android outlier — investigate separately"] |\n\nBy User Type / Cohort:\n| Metric | New Users | Returning Users | Power Users | Variance | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Metric] | [Value] | [Value] | [Value] | [+/-Z%] | [...] |\n\nBy Time Period:\n| Metric | Week 1 | Week 2 | Week 3 | Week 4 | Trend | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Metric] | [Value] | [Value] | [Value] | [Value] | ↑ ↓ → | [...] |\n\nSegment Insights:\n  - [e.g., "iOS users show 2.3x higher D7 retention than Android —\n    likely driven by notification prompt difference, not feature gap"]\n  - [e.g., "New user conversion rate is 4.1% vs 11.8% for returning\n    users — onboarding friction is disproportionately affecting\n    first-time users"]\n\n---\n\n7. RISKS AND FLAGS:\n⚠️ ESCALATIONS (High Likelihood + High Business Impact):\n- [Risk description — data-evidenced] | Owner: [Role] |\n  Action Required: [What must happen and by when]\n\nFull Risk and Flag Register:\n| Flag ID | Signal / Risk | Data Evidence | Likelihood | Business Impact | Score | Owner | Recommended Action | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F-01 | [e.g., "Crash rate trending toward SLA breach"] | [e.g., "Rate at 2.1% — SLA threshold is 2.5%"] | High/Med/Low | High/Med/Low | H-H / H-M etc. | [Role] | [Specific action — not just "monitor"] | Open / In Progress / Resolved |\n| F-02 | ... | ... | ... | ... | ... | ... | ... | ... |\n\n---\n\n8. RECOMMENDATIONS:\n[Every recommendation must be specific, actionable, and owned.\nDo not list generic suggestions. Prioritise by business impact.]\n\nPriority 1 — Immediate Actions (This Sprint / This Week):\n| Rec ID | Recommendation | Rationale | Owner | Deadline | Success Metric |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| REC-01 | [Specific action — e.g., "Revert push notification permission prompt to pre-v2.3 design and A/B test"] | [e.g., "Opt-out rate increase directly correlates with D7 retention drop"] | [Role] | [DD/MM/YYYY] | [e.g., "Opt-out rate returns to <25%"] |\n| REC-02 | ... | ... | ... | ... | ... |\n\nPriority 2 — Short-Term Actions (Next Sprint / Next 2 Weeks):\n| Rec ID | Recommendation | Rationale | Owner | Deadline | Success Metric |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| REC-03 | [...] | [...] | [Role] | [DD/MM/YYYY] | [...] |\n\nPriority 3 — Strategic / Longer-Term Actions:\n| Rec ID | Recommendation | Rationale | Owner | Deadline | Success Metric |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| REC-05 | [...] | [...] | [Role] | [DD/MM/YYYY] | [...] |\n\n---\n\n9. OPEN QUESTIONS:\n| Question | Why It Matters | Data Needed | Owner | Resolution Deadline |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Question 1 — e.g., "Is the Step 3 drop-off a UX issue or a data capture issue?"] | [e.g., "Determines whether fix is design or analytics instrumentation"] | [e.g., "Session recordings + funnel re-instrumentation"] | [Role] | [DD/MM/YYYY] |\n| [Question 2] | [...] | [...] | [Role] | [DD/MM/YYYY] |\n\n---\n\n10. NEXT REVIEW:\nNext Review Date: [DD/MM/YYYY]\nFocus Areas for Next Period:\n  - [e.g., "Validate impact of push notification prompt change on D7 retention"]\n  - [e.g., "First full Android dataset available — run platform parity analysis"]\n  - [e.g., "Confirm crash rate reduction following v2.4 hotfix release"]\n\nMetrics to Add or Retire:\n| Action | Metric | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Add | [e.g., "D30 Retention"] | [e.g., "D7 now stable — need longer-term signal"] |\n| Retire | [e.g., "Install source breakdowns"] | [e.g., "SKAdNetwork changes make this unreliable on iOS 17+"] |\n| [TARGET NEEDED] | [e.g., "LTV"] | [e.g., "Metric being tracked but no target defined — PO to set benchmark"] |\n\n---\n\nSUMMARY:\nREPORT PERIOD: [Period]\nDATA SOURCES: [N] | RECORDS ANALYSED: [N]\nKEY METRICS TRACKED: [N] | 🟢 ON TRACK: [N] | 🟡 AT RISK: [N] | 🔴 OFF TRACK: [N]\nFINDINGS: [N]\nRECOMMENDATIONS: [N] | IMMEDIATE: [N] | SHORT-TERM: [N] | STRATEGIC: [N]\nRISKS / FLAGS: [N] | ESCALATED: [N]\nDATA GAPS: [N] — Topics: [list]\nTARGETS NEEDED: [N] — Metrics: [list]\nOPEN QUESTIONS: [N]\nNEEDS CLARIFICATION FLAGS: [N] — Topics: [list]
27	7	Monitoring configuration prompts	ROLE:\nYou are a senior DevOps Engineer designing and documenting a Monitoring Configuration\nfor a Native iOS/Android mobile application in a production environment.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Both\nEnvironment: Dev | QA | Staging | Production\nMonitoring Tool(s): [e.g., Datadog | Firebase Crashlytics | New Relic | Grafana | Sentry | PagerDuty]\nApp Version: [Current Version]\nBackend Infrastructure: [e.g., AWS | GCP | Azure | On-premise]\nTeam Size: [Number of engineers on-call]\nOn-Call Rotation: Yes | No\nAlerting Channel: Slack | PagerDuty | Email | SMS | All\n\nTASK:\nUsing the application context and requirements below, produce a complete,\nproduction-ready Monitoring Configuration document.\nCover ALL of the following — do not skip any:\n  1. Monitoring objectives — what this configuration must detect and protect against\n  2. Key metrics to monitor — categorised by layer (app, backend, infrastructure)\n  3. Alert thresholds — specific values that trigger each alert\n  4. Alert severity levels — P1 through P4 with response time expectations\n  5. On-call and escalation policy — who gets alerted, when, and how\n  6. Dashboard configuration — what must be visible at a glance\n  7. Log configuration — what to log, at what level, and retention policy\n  8. Synthetic monitoring — uptime and user journey checks\n  9. Anomaly detection — where threshold-based alerts are insufficient\n  10. Runbooks — response steps for each critical alert\n\nFlag every value requiring environment-specific configuration as [CONFIG: VARIABLE_NAME].\nFlag any area where requirements are unclear as [NEEDS CLARIFICATION].\nFlag any metric where a baseline has not been established as [BASELINE NEEDED].\n\nAPPLICATION CONTEXT AND REQUIREMENTS:\n[Paste architecture overview, SLA requirements, previous incident history,\nor existing monitoring gaps here — or "Greenfield — no existing monitoring"]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Never hardcode credentials, API keys, or environment-specific endpoints\n- Every alert must have a defined owner role and response SLA\n- Thresholds must be based on data where possible — flag assumptions as [ASSUMPTION]\n- Do not create alert fatigue — every alert must require a human action\n- P1 alerts must have an immediate escalation path — do not rely on a single contact\n- Runbooks must be actionable — no vague steps like "investigate the issue"\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n\nMONITORING CONFIGURATION\nProject: [Project Name] | Platform: [Platform] | Environment: [Environment]\nPrepared By: [DevOps Engineer] | Date: [DD/MM/YYYY] | Tool(s): [Monitoring Tools]\n\n---\n\n1. MONITORING OBJECTIVES:\n  - [Objective 1 — e.g., "Detect crash rate spikes within 2 minutes of occurrence"]\n  - [Objective 2 — e.g., "Ensure API response time SLA of <200ms is maintained"]\n  - [Objective 3 — e.g., "Alert on-call engineer before users report an issue"]\n\nSLA Targets:\n| Metric | SLA Target | Breach Threshold | Review Frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., App Crash Rate] | [e.g., <1%] | [e.g., >2%] | [e.g., Real-time] |\n| [e.g., API Uptime] | [e.g., 99.9%] | [e.g., <99.5%] | [e.g., 5-min interval] |\n\n---\n\n2. KEY METRICS:\n\nApp Layer Metrics:\n| Metric | Description | Tool | Collection Interval | [BASELINE NEEDED] |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., Crash Rate] | [e.g., % of sessions ending in crash] | [e.g., Crashlytics] | [e.g., Real-time] | [e.g., <1%] |\n| [e.g., ANR Rate] | [e.g., App Not Responding events — Android only] | [e.g., Play Console] | [e.g., Daily] | [BASELINE NEEDED] |\n| [e.g., App Launch Time] | [e.g., Cold start duration in ms] | [e.g., Firebase Performance] | [e.g., Per session] | [e.g., <2000ms] |\n\nBackend / API Metrics:\n| Metric | Description | Tool | Collection Interval | Baseline |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., API Response Time] | [e.g., p95 latency per endpoint] | [e.g., Datadog] | [e.g., 1-min] | [e.g., <200ms] |\n| [e.g., Error Rate (5xx)] | [e.g., % of requests returning server errors] | [e.g., Datadog] | [e.g., 1-min] | [e.g., <0.5%] |\n| [e.g., Request Throughput] | [e.g., Requests per second] | [e.g., Grafana] | [e.g., 1-min] | [BASELINE NEEDED] |\n\nInfrastructure Metrics:\n| Metric | Description | Tool | Collection Interval | Baseline |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., CPU Utilisation] | [e.g., % CPU across app servers] | [e.g., CloudWatch] | [e.g., 5-min] | [e.g., <70%] |\n| [e.g., Memory Usage] | [e.g., % RAM used per instance] | [e.g., Datadog] | [e.g., 5-min] | [e.g., <80%] |\n| [e.g., Disk I/O] | [e.g., Read/write throughput on DB instances] | [e.g., Grafana] | [e.g., 5-min] | [BASELINE NEEDED] |\n\n---\n\n3. ALERT THRESHOLDS:\n| Alert ID | Metric | Condition | Warning Threshold | Critical Threshold | Severity | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A-01 | [e.g., Crash Rate] | [e.g., > threshold for 5 min] | [e.g., >1.5%] | [e.g., >2.5%] | [P2 / P1] | [Role] |\n| A-02 | [e.g., API Error Rate] | [e.g., > threshold for 2 min] | [e.g., >1%] | [e.g., >3%] | [P2 / P1] | [Role] |\n| A-03 | [e.g., App Launch Time] | [e.g., p95 > threshold] | [e.g., >3000ms] | [e.g., >5000ms] | [P3 / P2] | [Role] |\n\nThreshold Notes:\n  - [ASSUMPTION] [e.g., "Crash rate threshold based on industry benchmark —\n    adjust once 30-day baseline is established"]\n  - [BASELINE NEEDED] [e.g., "ANR rate baseline not yet established —\n    monitor for 2 sprints before setting alert threshold"]\n\n---\n\n4. ALERT SEVERITY LEVELS:\n| Severity | Label | Description | Response Time | Notification Channel | Escalation If Unacknowledged |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | Critical | [e.g., Production down or data loss] | [e.g., 5 minutes] | [e.g., PagerDuty + SMS + Slack] | [e.g., Escalate to Engineering Lead after 10 min] |\n| P2 | High | [e.g., Significant user impact — degraded service] | [e.g., 15 minutes] | [e.g., PagerDuty + Slack] | [e.g., Escalate to on-call lead after 30 min] |\n| P3 | Medium | [e.g., Minor impact — workaround available] | [e.g., 1 hour] | [e.g., Slack only] | [e.g., No auto-escalation — review in standup] |\n| P4 | Low | [e.g., No user impact — informational] | [e.g., Next business day] | [e.g., Email digest] | [e.g., No escalation required] |\n\n---\n\n5. ON-CALL AND ESCALATION POLICY:\nOn-Call Schedule:\n  - Primary On-Call: [Role] | Hours: [e.g., 24/7 | Business hours only]\n  - Secondary On-Call: [Role] | Escalation trigger: [e.g., No response within 10 min]\n  - Incident Commander: [Role] | Trigger: [e.g., P1 incidents only]\n\nEscalation Path:\n  - Level 1: [Role] — First responder | Response SLA: [N minutes]\n  - Level 2: [Role] — Engineering Lead | Triggered after: [N minutes unacknowledged]\n  - Level 3: [Role] — CTO / Head of Engineering | Triggered after: [N minutes unacknowledged]\n\n[CONFIG: ON_CALL_SCHEDULE] — Configure rotation in PagerDuty / OpsGenie before go-live.\n\n---\n\n6. DASHBOARD CONFIGURATION:\nPrimary Operations Dashboard (visible at all times):\n  - [e.g., Real-time crash rate — iOS and Android split]\n  - [e.g., API error rate and p95 latency — last 1 hour]\n  - [e.g., Active user count — last 15 minutes]\n  - [e.g., Current alert status — P1 and P2 only]\n\nRelease Health Dashboard (active during and after deployments):\n  - [e.g., Crash rate delta — new version vs prior version]\n  - [e.g., API error rate — 30 min pre and post release]\n  - [e.g., App launch time — new version vs baseline]\n\nBusiness Metrics Dashboard (reviewed daily):\n  - [e.g., DAU / MAU trend — last 30 days]\n  - [e.g., Session duration — platform split]\n  - [e.g., Conversion funnel drop-off — last 7 days]\n\n---\n\n7. LOG CONFIGURATION:\n| Log Type | Source | Log Level | Retention Period | Storage |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., App crash logs] | [e.g., Crashlytics] | [e.g., ERROR] | [e.g., 90 days] | [e.g., Firebase / S3] |\n| [e.g., API access logs] | [e.g., API Gateway] | [e.g., INFO + ERROR] | [e.g., 30 days] | [e.g., CloudWatch] |\n| [e.g., Infrastructure logs] | [e.g., EC2 / ECS] | [e.g., WARN + ERROR] | [e.g., 14 days] | [e.g., Datadog] |\n\nLog Standards:\n  - [e.g., All logs must include: timestamp, environment, app version, user_id (hashed), request_id]\n  - [e.g., PII must never appear in logs — mask before writing]\n  - [e.g., Structured JSON logging required for all backend services]\n\n---\n\n8. SYNTHETIC MONITORING:\n| Check ID | Check Type | Target | Frequency | Failure Threshold | Alert Severity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| S-01 | [e.g., Uptime ping] | [e.g., /health endpoint] | [e.g., Every 1 min] | [e.g., 2 consecutive failures] | [P1] |\n| S-02 | [e.g., User journey] | [e.g., Login → Dashboard flow] | [e.g., Every 5 min] | [e.g., >5s or error] | [P2] |\n| S-03 | [e.g., API contract check] | [e.g., POST /api/v1/auth] | [e.g., Every 5 min] | [e.g., Non-200 response] | [P2] |\n\n---\n\n9. ANOMALY DETECTION:\n[Use anomaly detection where static thresholds are insufficient — e.g., traffic\npatterns that vary significantly by time of day or day of week.]\n\n| Metric | Anomaly Type | Detection Method | Sensitivity | Alert Severity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., API request volume] | [e.g., Sudden drop — may indicate client-side crash] | [e.g., Datadog anomaly detection — seasonal algorithm] | [e.g., Medium] | [P2] |\n| [e.g., New user registrations] | [e.g., Spike — may indicate bot activity] | [e.g., 3-sigma deviation from 7-day rolling average] | [e.g., Low] | [P3] |\n\n---\n\n10. RUNBOOKS:\n\nRUNBOOK: High Crash Rate (A-01)\nTrigger: Crash rate exceeds critical threshold for 5 consecutive minutes\nOwner: [On-call Engineer Role]\nSteps:\n  1. Check Crashlytics for crash grouping — identify top crash type and affected version\n  2. Determine if crash is isolated to a specific OS version, device type, or app version\n  3. If new app version: initiate rollback procedure — see [LINK TO ROLLBACK RUNBOOK]\n  4. If existing version: assess user impact — escalate to P1 if >5% of sessions affected\n  5. Open incident ticket in JIRA — link to crash report\n  6. Notify stakeholders via Slack [CONFIG: INCIDENT_SLACK_CHANNEL] if P1 declared\n  7. Deploy hotfix if root cause identified — follow emergency release process\n\nRUNBOOK: API Error Rate Spike (A-02)\nTrigger: API 5xx error rate exceeds critical threshold for 2 consecutive minutes\nOwner: [On-call Engineer Role]\nSteps:\n  1. Check API Gateway logs for error distribution across endpoints\n  2. Identify if errors are isolated to a single endpoint or service-wide\n  3. Check recent deployments — correlate spike timing with release log\n  4. If database issue suspected: check connection pool and query performance\n  5. If third-party dependency: check vendor status page and open support ticket\n  6. Escalate to P1 if error rate exceeds 5% or payment endpoints are affected\n  7. Communicate status to stakeholders if incident duration exceeds 15 minutes\n\n[Add one runbook per critical alert — do not leave P1 alerts without a runbook]\n\n---\n\nCONFIGURATION NOTES:\n\nSecrets and Variables Required (configure before activation):\n| Variable Name | Description | Where to Set |\n|---|---|---|\n| [CONFIG: ON_CALL_SCHEDULE] | [On-call rotation schedule] | [PagerDuty / OpsGenie] |\n| [CONFIG: INCIDENT_SLACK_CHANNEL] | [Slack channel for P1/P2 alerts] | [Monitoring tool webhook settings] |\n| [CONFIG: ALERT_EMAIL_LIST] | [Distribution list for P3/P4 alerts] | [Monitoring tool notification settings] |\n\nPrerequisites:\n  - [e.g., Monitoring tool API key configured in CI/CD secrets]\n  - [e.g., Crashlytics SDK integrated in app — minimum version X.X]\n  - [e.g., On-call rotation confirmed and tested before production go-live]\n\nEstimated Setup Time: [N hours]\nReview Frequency: [e.g., Thresholds reviewed every sprint | Quarterly full review]\n\nNotes:\n  - [ASSUMPTION] [Any threshold or configuration choice based on assumption rather than data]\n  - [NEEDS CLARIFICATION] [Any area requiring team input before configuration is finalised]
